WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hi  i'm galila and until recently  i did not know how to fire up a stove or  operate an oven and the reason for that  is a story  that goes back a couple of generations  it started in the highlands of ethiopia  in a small village called  debra brahan my grandmother lived there  ever since she could remember her entire  life revolved around  her husband and her eight children  my grandmother wanted it to be different  for her daughter  so she went against the prevailing norms  at the time  and she sent her first daughter to  school  my mother became the first woman  in the entire history of my family to be  educated  but although she went to school she did  not have enough time to study  because as a woman she still had  obligations in the kitchen  my mother wanted it to be different for  her daughter me  she believed in education in the same  way that she believed in the ten  commandments  and so she refused point blank to let me  enter the kitchen  instead pushed me to aspire to a life  filled with intellectual and creative  pursuits  this is the reason why i'm here today  also the reason why until recently  i could only make toast in the kitchen  for some time i've been asking myself  this question  why did it take generations for us to  re-examine social norms and change them  why did it take over 50 years could it  be done  quicker within the span of our own lives  i'd like to tell you my story and the  progress i've made thus far and trying  to answer this question  so when i think about my story and how i  learned the values in my community  i think of lego pieces  as a kid i learned the norms and values  of my community  through osmosis piece by piece from  dinner table conversations folklores and  bedtime stories from sunday church  sermons  and school  like most kids i would ask why about  things that didn't make sense to me  or questions about culture  religion why men and women were treated  differently  and the story about the gender dynamics  in my family  was the core foundational piece of my  lego block of values  as you can imagine the answer i got  usually was  i don't know kid it's always been done  this way which is cute for me  to close a chapter on that discussion  and  move on  but i still had questions  in my mind and i would mark them as red  flags that  were unresolved and as i grew older the  number of red flags increased the number  until i had a long list of red flags  at this point it became clear to me that  if i picked up a lego piece  to question and examine it the entire  structure  might completely unravel this was major  cognitive dissonance for me  so here i was apparently i was supposed  to ask engage  question science and math problems in  school but when it came to social things  the things that mattered my values why i  am who i  am why we do things we do  there is no room for critical thinking  so  my kid rain deduced that there must be  two types of ideas in this world  the ideas that you can question  relegated to the scientific realm and  mathematics  and ideas you just accept and faith  but i knew that this way of thinking was  a shortcoming for me  because i noticed i would get emotional  and defensive if anyone tried to  question my lego of values  i could not explain why i believe the  things i did and i could not hold my own  in any debate  then i entered an entirely  new chapter in my life when i moved from  adi sabawa to new york for college  in the dorms in manhattan with an ocean  between me  and my hometown i could take a step back  and take the perspective of an outsider  looking in  you know how monks go off to far-off  caves to  read books and reflect on things this  was my version of that  i poured through ancient texts like  siddhartha  and plato's republic and foundational  texts like the bible  quran i and  the federalist papers written by the  founding fathers of america  i came to a realization that these books  were  essentially giving us the organizing  principle for building our lego of  values  my most meaningful  intellectual breakthrough happened when  i realized  that these books were written by people  who lived hundreds of years ago but  still people  who are no different than me or you  if they can create norms for their time  why shouldn't anybody  should we just take what they've given  us  without questioning this then made me  realize that  i needed to take ownership of my own  value system i needed to  think through what matters to me  so  i started examining my lego values piece  by piece  turning them around rearranging dusting  them off  and some of them some that i  didn't need would chuck them away in the  old and archaic category  effectively murray condoing my values  in this process i came to realize that  the underlying theme  among all of our red flags was this  it was structured this way because you  are x you should not do y  because you are a woman young  black ethiopian christian you should not  do  and the list goes on  my discovery was that to remove red  flags  all that was required was to ask why and  in that process  the fundamental problem in the societal  norm  becomes revealed and that is what the  red flag is covering in the first place  for example in my case  one of my red flags was this because you  are a woman  you should not travel or live on your  own  so after going through the process and  asking why  several times i realized that this came  from a place  of care and protection but  putting artificial constraints  on my movement or my degree of freedom  was not going to solve the root problem  and the problem was that men and women  were treated differently  throughout history and that has resulted  in all sorts of consequences  and so by putting constraints on myself  i wasn't going to solve this problem  so effectively the red flag needed to go  as a first step i moved into my own dorm  and started living on my own  and then i moved into the city got an  apartment by myself  as for traveling i booked a bus ticket  and i did a solo trip solo weekend trip  by myself  and then after a few months i booked  plane tickets  and traveled on my own 25 different  cities around the world  as an aside just like to say to my  parents there's a good reason why i  didn't tell you this  um please forgive me and i will explain  okay going back to my red flags so  in this process i went through resolved  a bunch of red flags  and also learned that red flags  if left unresolved become what our  professors here at stanford call  limiting beliefs or gremlins of the past  that unconsciously hold us back  after many years of critical analysis i  understand why  it's difficult for many of us to go  through this process  and to constantly examine our values  it's challenging it's not pleasant at  all i had nuts in my stomach  every time i tried to go through this  process but  after going through this process is that  i can trace back  where all my values come from and  i have full conviction on  everything that i stand for which is  incredibly empowering  and if we don't go through this process  of  constantly re-examining our values it'll  take us  a long time a whole generation  for us to find inconsistencies in our  communal values  and to find red flags  so my ask is this if any of this  resonated with you  i encourage you to set time  on your calendar once every month  at the end of each month to dust off  your lego values and find red flags  for all the norms in our community  that don't spark joy let's take a moment  to acknowledge them as part of our  history  and say goodbye  and if you know of a kid in your life  brother sister siblings child  nephews encourage them to ask why  so that they so that they are actively  involved  in understanding the norms and values in  the community  let them ask as they build so  they don't have a lego castle that they  need to break down when they're much  older  like me encourage them to ask why  and to be actively involved in  understanding these norms and values  so that they can build from the ground  up and they can build their foundations  as they're at a very young age  i will leave you with this poem  when i was starting off on this journey  a dear friend gave me a poem  that has inspired me to continue on this  path  and it goes like this  there's a sun within every person one of  these days at dawn  you will rise within because you  are a child of the sun  unlike the moon that gets its energy  from other sources  the sun radiates from within and creates  its own energy  like the sun you create your own energy  your own story because the outside world  has no business defining it for you  [Music]

